 the  the other contributor or contributors  might be a 4-6 or even a 4-4  but what if i gave you more context and  said that there's only one other  contributor you may then say okay we can  make some pretty confident assumptions  that this is  a a 3 4 mixed with a 4 6.  but massively parallel sequencing lets  us take this a little bit further and  say i can actually tell you what part of  that 4 peak  came from our victim and what part of  that four peak  came from our person of interest and  this is done using the sequence level  information  that is contained in each of those  triangles so we can break apart  um that four triangle into two pieces  the red part that may belong to the  victim for example and the blue part  that may belong to our person of  interest  so now moving into some of the actual  studies that we're doing in the context  of mixtures  and some of these more complex dna  profiles  so first i want to introduce um the sort  of rationale behind why  we have started predicting str genotypes  from identity informative snips  so um i've i've presented both of these  types of loci on the last couple of  slides  um we know that strs are highly  polymorphic we know that they have  larger amplicon sizes but they do tend  to suffer from pcr artifacts  and this makes them especially  difficult to really get good information  from  in the context of a compromised  substrate such as human remains  or a substrate that may be heavily  environmentally insulted from something  like humic acids  on the contrary the snips are smaller  amplicons so so degradation may not be  as much of an issue  they don't have pcr artifacts so  untangling stutter from true peaks may  not be a problem  they are also less polymorphic so we do  need many more of them  um but they are easier to type and  compromise substrates  so much of the approach to tackling sort  of challenging human remains or  challenging environmentally  salted samples has been from the side of  the chemistry  we've taken a different approach and  said what if we just use the data that  we can get from the identity informative  snips  and better use the data that we can get  from this profile  so to do this uh we rely on a process  called imputation  and this is basically a statistical  inference of unobserved genotypes  at the str loci of interest using  identity informative sniff loci of  interest  so this relies heavily on the concept of  linkage disequilibrium between  snips and strs in  a diagram on the right side of the  screen i'm showing sort of roughly how  this works  um if we have our study samples that  contain our snip haplotype of interest  we also have a set of reference  information that includes  typically whole genome sequenced  reference panels  that include our strs in the black box  and our snips of interest  all in the same read and then we can  basically perform a vlookup or look up  our haplotype  in our reference and say okay this  matches and then i can put a weight  to how well it matches and what i think  the  gaps are in the str locus  so we performed this process  in a couple ways and i'll show that on  the next two slides  but first we identified strs that were  in high linkage disequilibrium with  snips of interest from the forensic dna  signature prep kit  um we evaluated their heterozygosities  we compared some  random match probability information and  then we're also investigating a leave  one out analysis  to investigate how some incompleteness  of your ii snip profile  may actually contribute to the data that  you can receive  in forms of str genotypes  so to very quickly summarize uh why  we're doing this  str's likely won't work well in the  context of compromised human remains  uh snips could work a little bit better  uh partial snip profiles  may actually in in our study be  predictive  of partial sdr profiles such that the  random match probability could become a  little bit better  and then ultimately the long term here  is to test whether or not what we're  doing in the computer actually reflects  what happens  in real life  so here is a uh study overview we took  two  sort of sets of snips the first was uh  an entire  snipper raise information of like you  know several million snips  uh we also then reduced that snip count  to only the 94 identity informative  snips from the forensic dna  signature prep kit we performed snipster  imputation and then we compared  the heterozygosities and allele counts  for three sets of strs the codus loci  the navroski at all 73 plex and the  highly concordant strs that we  identified through this procedure  so this work is primarily been  spearheaded by angelica who is a  master's student in nicole navroski's  lab at the university of toronto  she took these 222 snips which  represents the 94 ii snip amplicons  we received just over or sorry just  under  170 000 strs imputed  67 000 of those had global  heterozygositys greater than zero  and 24 000 of those had heterozygosities  greater than 25  in uh three major global population  groups of interest  we did use the thousand genomes project  for this  and we asked two primary questions  what's going on with codis  and the navroski at all 73 plex and then  what's going on with the other loci that  we found that were actually imputed  really well  so first um under ideal conditions so  when we gave the procedure the entire  snip arrays worth of information  um the codis loci and the nebroski at  all 73 plex  generally imputed pretty well so when we  compared this  to the imputation using just the ii  snips we only identified three snips  or sorry three strs that actually gave  us some meaningful information in terms  of amputation success  in terms of concordance with the  heterozygosity and allele spread with  the the truth data  these include one of the nebraska at all  loci and then two of the codus loci  listed here  what's more interesting is that we  actually found 545 str's that were well  imputed using our 94 iii snips  i'm showing here the heterozygosities of  those  strs in three of our population groups  using the ii snip  and the snip array information those are  pretty similar really nice overlap and  the allele spreads are actually  perfect between the two of them so our  next steps  um because the heterozygosities of these  loci were quite low they're probably not  super useful for hid so human identity  purposes  so we are currently characterizing their  ancestry effects as well as some of the  phenotypic consequences of these  554 or sorry 545 strs  this manuscript is in preparation uh  with angelica taking the lead  um under the mentorship of nicole broski  so next we'll look at simulating  mixtures um  very briefly what does it mean to  simulate data we actually do all of this  on the computer so we can take  real-world scenarios and then  make data out of these real-world  scenarios  the value of doing this is that we have  a lot of control over what conditions  affect the data that we produce this is  both time  and cost effective and scales to very  large sample sizes  which is really really great for early  career investigators like myself  one of the major limitations to this  that i will be super  transparent about throughout the  presentation is whether or not there is  some sampling error or stochasticity  in how these data are simulated  so i'm showing here also a picture of an  undergraduate student ashley  in nicole navrati's lab she has been  instrumental in putting all of this work  together  so i do also want to make sure that she  gets the the shout out that she deserves  so we used the r package forensim that  was developed  as a set of tools to study and interpret  dna mixtures  we used this package to simulate  genotypes and mixtures and population  data based on published allele  frequencies  the package also allows you to perform  some statistical interpretation of the  data that you generate  we'll be focusing on the likelihood  ratios but there are a whole bunch of  other  statistics that you can generate in  forensim  so our overall goal was to assess the  reproducibility and dementability of  forensim to large population genetic and  mixture studies  we relied on the population allele  frequency data from nabroski at all in  2016  using african-american asian-american  european-american and hispanic  population groups  all of which have been genotyped for  strs in the  forensic dna signature prep kit  our study design included generating 50  population replicates of each of our  target populations for a total of 38  900 individuals um we performed a couple  of population genetic parameters on  these uh 50 replicates and then compared  those back to  the nicole naroski estimates we then  took  all of the individuals that we were  studying  and created 2400 same ancestry mixtures  what that means is that we took two  people of african-american  from the african-american cohort mixed  them together and then performed our  mixture matched statistics using those  individuals  so our primary end point here was the  likelihood ratios  and we did compare those back to uh the  same likelihood ratio or sorry the  likelihood ratio for the same profile  calculated with nabroski at all  um and i do want to point out here that  for  simplicity all of our likelihood ratios  assume the most  basic uh  numerator and denominator so we  essentially said that all of the alleles  observed in the profile can be explained  by  this person versus cannot be explained  by that person  so in the context of a more complicated  or several alternative hypotheses  much of the data that i present today  may not hold  so looking at our population genetic  information we're showing here in panel  a the distribution of hardy-weinberg  equilibrium p-values  across our four american population  groups  we see the vertical dashed line  indicates the nominally significant  p-value of 0.05  so everything to the left of that line  indicates a significant deviation from  hardy-weinberg equilibrium  at least at the nominally significant  level we tabulate what those  deviations look like across all of the  strs  in the forensic panel in object b  here we see that there is really no  indication that a specific locus  is causing a problem here and actually  when we perform our multiple testing  correction  we still see that this is not really any  indicator that a specific str is giving  us issues in terms of our simulated  population data  unfortunately our pattern of significant  deviations from hardy-weinberg  equilibrium  also look very different from what uh  navroski had all observed  in the 2016 article  in terms of allele frequencies i'm  showing here  in black the point estimates in the 95  confidence intervals from nebraska at  all  in red blue purple and green i'm showing  the point estimates  from our 50 simulated populations you'll  see that all of those distributions do  fall within the 95  confidence intervals of navrovsky at all  and this was initially really exciting  to us  but as we thought about it a little bit  more um  we became curious about how these even  minor changes  in allele frequency distribution could  impact the resulting match  statistics for either single source  profiles or a mixed dna profile  so we went on to test how that may  affect  what our mixture statistics look like so  here we have our simulated mixture  results  for two-person three-person and  four-person mixtures again we are only  using same ancestry  uh combinations of individuals so in the  top left there that afa  box we are only looking at two-person  mixtures  both of whom are of african-american uh  or both of whom are from our  african-american cohort  we took that mixture and we calculated  likelihood ratios for all 200 of the  mixtures we created  and those likelihood ratios were derived  using allele frequencies from all four  populations  and what we're showing here is that  in every instance the true contributor  population is  the one producing the least prejudicial  um likelihood ratios and this is a  really good sign this was more of a  sanity check for us  um to make sure that the mixtures we  created and the populations we generated  are giving us estimates that we expect  to see in the real world  i will point out that as we move into  that hispanic column  we do see that a a relatively large  proportion of the non-contributor  likelihood ratios fall below that  diagonal dashed line  indicating that the least prejudicial  estimate of the likelihood ratio  actually came from  a non-contributor population group  and i think that this ultimately has  some pretty major implications for  mixtures including highly admixed  population groups  so here we're showing how your choice of  population replicate influences the  distribution of likelihood ratios across  all 200 of the mixtures that we created  per facet or per group  of individuals so on the x-axis we're  showing the standardized mean difference  and this is really just a way to  better capture the magnitude of effect  size between two distributions  given the standard deviation across  everybody from those two  uh groups on the y-axis we're looking at  the  minus log 10 p-value for the fdr  adjusted p-value  so the higher the data point the more  significant the difference between  um our distribution of likelihood ratios  and nicole nabroski's distribution of  likelihood ratios  so what you'll notice here is that first  and foremost there are a lot of data  points in all four of those facets that  are above the dashed line  this indicates that we saw a ton of  significant differences when compared to  the  distributions from nebraska at all i do  also want to point out here that with  the exception of the asian american  population over half of all of the other  population groups  produced a significantly different  likelihood ratio distribution  um which is suggesting to us that you  know your  your choice of replicate and and even  just using one replicate  may not be very sufficient in terms of  accurately modeling what you think  should happen with the data that you've  truly genotyped  i do also want to point out here that in  the eu a  facet that's the european americans we  see three populations that we simulated  that actually had  extremely large effect sizes or  standardized mean differences sorry  and extremely significant p values so  all of these negative  data points on the x-axis indicate that  our simulate simulated populations  produced larger uh log-10 likelihood  ratios so the the profile was actually  indicated as being more rare than it  would have been in the nebraska at all  estimate  so that's fine but do those results  uh suggest that maybe we have an issue  with just a couple of mixtures that went  into the analyses  so here we're showing the uh  how the number of replicate populations  chosen  influences the likelihood ratio  distributions per  uh per mixture so each data point here  represents a mixture  those are shown on the left side of each  violin plot whereas the actual  distribution of data is shown on the  right side of each violin plot  on the x-axis we're showing the number  of replicates that was included  in the calculation of the mean  likelihood ratio  on the y axis we're showing the  difference between that mean  and the nebraska at all estimate um  as we increase the number of replicates  this is unsurprising  that we sort of reduce the distribution  of mean differences  thankfully it's being reduced to  hovering around zero suggesting that  there is little to no difference between  um the replicated populations and the  nebroski at all populations  however um i'm only showing the the afa  subset here just to  to make the point more clear um  after multiple testing correction we  identify that this  choice of 10 replicate populations going  into your mixture study  is where we seem to converge on uh the  narrowest distribution  relative to the original distribution so  the means from 10 replicates onward tend  to hover around zero and those  distributions  have the smallest variance relative to  uh just picking one replicate  so you'll see here that the actual  distribution of differences in that one  replicate  violin plot are not super large so for  two person  mixtures this may not be super  meaningful to include a bunch of  replicates  although we still think that  statistically this is a significant  observation  when we look at the i believe these are  the three person mixtures  we see a very different story we are  still identifying 10 replicates  as being a really good spot for us to to  you know  target in terms of how many times should  we ascertain this data  however what we're not seeing is that  narrowing of the distribution  all of our means do tend to reduce  closer to zero  but we still have a substantial number  of individuals on the extreme ends of  our distribution  such that the variance does not change  among these  uh these observations so in some  instances so maybe these three and four  person mixtures  um we have not actually identified an  appropriate number of replicates yet  with which to sort of get a meaningful  useful and high confidence estimate of  what's actually going on  so to summarize here  when using modeling tools it's really  important to  uh pay attention to some comprehensive  assessments of the reliability of those  tools  and really the impetus behind us doing  this project is that we didn't feel like  we had  literature for that to support our use  of friend  in some of these projects that we want  to do so here we demonstrated that the  forensim data works really well or sorry  the the forensin package works really  well with the nebraska at all data  it is fast it is efficient we can look  at really large sample sizes  but we have not demonstrated that our  observations that i presented you  to you today can 